==Aftermath==
When highlights from the match were shown on British television a couple of days later (not the same night, because film of matches had to be flown back to the UK), the match was introduced by BBC sports commentator [[David Coleman]] as "the most stupid, appalling, disgusting and disgraceful exhibition of football, possibly in the history of the game."<ref name="sbrntn180322th"/><ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T5jVMSlpZhg Italy v Chile World Cup 1962 The Battle of Santiago &ndash; YouTube (via broodje80).] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220122124200/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T5jVMSlpZhg |date=22 January 2022 }} Retrieved January 22, 2022.</ref>

Coleman also observed that it was the first meeting between the sides and "we hope it will be the last."<ref name="sbrntn180322th"/> However, the sides were drawn together at the [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966 World Cup]] and met at [[Sunderland AFC|Sunderland]]'s [[Roker Park]] ground with Italy winning 2&ndash;0. The rematch also featured unsportsmanlike play but to a lesser degree.<ref>[https://www.sunderlandecho.com/heritage-and-retro/heritage/when-the-world-cup-came-to-roker-park-and-it-ended-in-disaster-for-italy-3302459 Gillan, Tony. "When the World Cup came to Roker Park and it ended in disaster for Italy," ''Sunderland Echo'', Sunday 11 July 2021.] Retrieved January 22, 2022.</ref>

